# Booking Tracker — Working Rules

Short, practical rules for working on this project with Claude / Claude Code / Cowork.
No PR pipeline, no multi-role framework — just enough structure to avoid repeating mistakes.

## Before starting any task
- Read `STATUS.md` (and this file) first. Don't guess where the project stands.
- Confirm the ONE specific task to do now. Don't "continue where we left off" — state it explicitly.

## While working
- Make the smallest change that solves the task. No unrelated refactoring "while I'm in there."
- If a rule or convention already exists (see "Known rules" below), follow it — don't re-derive it.
- If you hit the same bug a second or third time without fixing it, STOP. Say so plainly, explain the likely root cause, and don't just try another small patch blind.

## After a change
- Summarize: what changed, what was deliberately NOT touched, what should be manually tested (there is no automated test suite — Harry tests by hand).
- Update `STATUS.md`: what works now, what's still broken/open.
- Record any new decision or rule directly in this file, immediately — not just in chat.

## Known rules (keep this list updated)
- `seedDefaultsIfEmpty()` must always stay disabled.
- All form labels must be in English.
- Correct partner names: Albatros Travel, Asien Travel, DANexplore, Unique Travel.
- Auth (2026-09-18 decision): real Firebase Auth (email/password) + Firestore Security Rules with an explicit employee-email allowlist, NOT the earlier abandoned Google Sign-In popup/redirect approach (that was unreliable on GitHub Pages). No public self-registration; Harry creates every account manually in Firebase Console. Built and tested (see STATUS.md), not deployed yet — do not assume it's live without checking STATUS.md.
- Access control (2026-09-18, superseded same day): first built with an admin-only-delete split (only Harry could delete). Harry then explicitly asked for this to be simplified: there is NO separate admin role — every allowlisted account has the same rights (read/create/update/delete). Harry's control point is the allowlist itself (who has an account at all), not a permission tier inside the app. Don't reintroduce an admin/delete distinction without Harry asking again.
- Presence ("who's online"): a `presence` Firestore collection, one doc per user keyed by their Firebase Auth uid, updated via a 45s heartbeat. A user counts as online if their heartbeat is under 2 minutes old — no cleanup job needed, stale docs just age out of the count.
- My Action List (2026-09-18, built, not yet deployed — see STATUS.md): new default landing tab, personal task queue. Reuses existing status/sent/reply/deposit/creditcard fields, no new data model for follow-up/payment. New additive fields: `h.assignedAgent` (escalation target, overrides `booking.agent` for that one hotel line) and `h.escalations[]` (trail). New `users` Firestore collection (email -> displayName/agentName/role) manages the login-email-to-agent-name mapping; `role: 'admin'` ONLY controls the "show everyone's tasks" toggle on My Action List — it is NOT the old delete-permission admin tier (that stays removed, see the access-control decision above) and does not affect delete rights. Fallback admin (before `users` is populated) is hardcoded to brian.harry.boysen@gmail.com only. `USER_DISPLAY_NAMES` was corrected at the same time to the real employee names Harry supplied (Harry, Kai, New, Natty, Sherly, Benz, Waan, Mi, Hem, Nipon, Bee, Air, Jonas, Winnie, Neung) — it previously had generic placeholder names. No firestore.rules changes needed (generic wildcard rule already covers any new collection).
- Bookings tab is no longer the default active tab (My Action List is). Any new Playwright test that opens the "New booking" modal must first click `[data-tab="bookings"]`.
- Rule-based follow-up + data validation (2026-09-18, built, not yet deployed — see STATUS.md): follow-up severity (neutral/amber/red) is computed only from `h.sent`/`h.reply`/`h.status`, never a manual status word, using `daysSince()`/`daysBetween()` which are now Thailand-time-based (`thailandTodayStr()`, Asia/Bangkok, no DST) instead of browser-local time — don't reintroduce raw `new Date()` day-diff math for anything follow-up-related. Thresholds live in Firestore `settings/followup` ({amberThresholdDays, redThresholdDays}, default 2/4), editable in the "(team)" modal — never hardcode new threshold values elsewhere. Booking form now requires guest name and Partner (Partner used to say "Optional" — it no longer is); validation errors are shown inline next to the field via `markFieldError()`/`clearFieldErrors()`, not `alert()`. Check-out must be strictly after check-in (same-day is now invalid, not just earlier) — the smart-default now suggests check-in+1 day, not same-day. Partner stays a `<select>` bound to the Partner master data on purpose; Agent still uses a datalist autocomplete (`agentSuggestions`) plus case-insensitive normalization on save (`normalizeAgainstList()`) so "sherly"/"Sherly" don't fork into two records — Hotel/supplier name and Room Type were upgraded past autocomplete to real dropdowns, see the next bullet. Duplicate-booking warning (same ref, or same guest + shared check-in date) always shows via `#duplicateModal`; only `role: 'admin'` (the same narrow My Action List role, not the old delete-admin tier) gets a "Save anyway" override — everyone else is hard-blocked. `booking.history[]` is an append-only change log (who/when/what) shown under the hotels table in the expanded Bookings-tab card.
- Hotel/Supplier + Room Type as real dropdowns (2026-09-18, built, not yet deployed — see STATUS.md): Hotel/Supplier Name is now a `<select class="hf-hotel-select">` (like Partner) sourced from `knownHotelNames()`, with a "+ Add new hotel..." option (`value="__new"`) that reveals `<input class="hf-name-new">`. A hotel picked via "+ Add new hotel..." is auto-persisted as a new independent `hotelsCrm` doc on save (same schema as `saveCrmEntity()`) — this is the actual mechanism that prevents one hotel forking into two spellings, since it becomes a real selectable option for every future booking. Room Type is the same pattern, per hotel-line: `<select class="hf-rt-select">` + `<input class="hf-rt-name-new">`, sourced from `knownRoomTypeNamesForHotel(hotelName)` (that hotel's own Allotment + past bookings, case-insensitive; falls back to the full cross-hotel list when the hotel has no room-type history yet), rebuilt via `refreshRoomTypeLineOptions(block)` whenever the hotel selection changes. Deliberate asymmetry: unlike hotels, a new room type picked via "+ Add new room type..." is NEVER auto-written into an allotment's `roomTypes` array — room types have no independent master record, and inserting a bare `{name, qty:0}`-shaped entry into an existing allotment doc risks corrupting the Full/Low badge and sweep-line peak-occupancy math. Room types only get the same `normalizeAgainstList()` safety net on save. Both `saveBooking()`'s hotel-block loop and its `roomTypeFilled`/`hasAnyData` checks read from `.hf-hotel-select`/`.hf-name-new` and `.hf-rt-select`/`.hf-rt-name-new` — never reintroduce a plain free-text `.hf-name` or `.hf-rt-name` input, and any Playwright test driving these fields must use `page.selectOption()` (`.fill()` throws on a `<select>`), picking `'__new'` first when typing a brand-new name.
- `syncHotelsFromBookings()` (2026-09-18, built, not yet deployed — see STATUS.md): a button in the Hotels CRM view ("Add all hotels from bookings", visible only when `crmType==='hotels'`) that backfills a `hotelsCrm` doc for every name in `knownHotelNames()` not already present. Purely additive (never overwrites/deletes), so — unlike the rename/delete actions below — it's open to everyone, not admin-gated. Safe to run repeatedly (case-insensitive existence check, no duplicates).
- Admin-only rename/merge/delete for hotels and room types (2026-09-18, built, not yet deployed — see STATUS.md): a SCOPED exception to the "no admin tier" access-control decision above — it applies ONLY to renaming/deleting an EXISTING hotel (`hotelsCrm` record or an allotment's `hotelName`) or an EXISTING room type inside an allotment, because those actions rewrite historical booking data; it does NOT reopen the general admin/delete question, and does NOT restrict adding a brand-new hotel or room type (still open to everyone). Gated by the same narrow `isAdminViewer()` used elsewhere (My Action List's admin toggle, duplicate-warning override) — not a new role. Shared cascade helpers: `countHotelUsage(name)` / `countRoomTypeUsage(hotelName, roomTypeName)` (usage counts shown before any admin confirm), `renameHotelEverywhere(oldName, newName, opts)` and `renameRoomTypeEverywhere(hotelName, oldName, newName)` (rewrite every matching booking's hotel-line/room-type text, plus allotments/hotelsCrm for the hotel case — never silent, always logs a `booking.history[]` entry). Rename-detection compares the OLD vs NEW name as an exact trimmed string (not case-insensitive) — a pure case fix (e.g. "rawi warin resort" → "Rawi Warin Resort") must still trigger the cascade/merge, since that's precisely the kind of duplicate this exists to fix; `renameHotelEverywhere`'s own internal matching stays case-insensitive so it still finds the old spelling wherever it appears. If renaming a `hotelsCrm` record collides with another existing record of the new name, they're merged (notes combined) and the now-duplicate record deleted, rather than left as two cards with the same name. UI: `#crm-name` (CRM Hotels modal) and `#allot-hotel`/`.rt-name` (Allotment modal) are `disabled` for non-admin when editing something that already has a saved name (`openCrmModal`/`openAllotmentModal`/`addRoomTypeBlock` check `isAdminViewer()`); adding a brand-new hotel/room type (blank field, no prior name) stays unlocked for everyone. `removeRoomTypeBlock(btn)` replaced the old bare `.remove()` on the room-type "×" button — a never-saved blank line still removes freely, but removing an existing one requires admin + shows a usage-count confirm.

## Model choice
- Default to the standard model (Sonnet) for normal fixes and features.
- Only escalate to a heavier model for genuinely hard structural decisions (e.g. finally solving the login/auth problem properly).

